  "scenario": "A buyer eats one or two squares of dark chocolate most evenings and wonders whether replacing a roughly two-dollar supermarket bar with a ten-to-twelve-dollar craft bean-to-bar habit would produce a taste improvement large enough to justify the recurring premium. The dollar amounts in that premise are the buyer's anchors, not verified current prices. The frozen packet instead contains five exact seller snapshots: a 25.45-dollar twelve-pack of 3.30-ounce DOVE dark bars; a 30-dollar twelve-pack of 3.5-ounce Lindt 70-percent bars; one 8.99-dollar 100-gram Ritter Sport 74-percent bar; a 21.99-dollar three-pack of 100-gram Green & Black 70-percent bars; and a 14.95-dollar three-pack of 1.8-ounce Raaka coconut-milk 60-percent bars whose page calls the product bean-to-bar, small-batch, single-origin, organic and directly sourced. The Raaka title implies 5.4 ounces across three bars while Product Dimensions reports 1.8 ounces, so its delivered total requires verification. Background pages define bean-to-bar as control of production from bean procurement to finished chocolate, distinguish dry cocoa solids from broader legal uses of cocoa solids, describe bulk-versus-flavor cocoa as partly subjective and processing-sensitive, define Fairtrade cocoa as a certified price-support scheme, separate analytical sensory difference from affective liking, describe the nine-point hedonic scale, and explain how blinding limits expectation bias. Three community pages contain only scoped anecdotes about a bar-or-two daily habit, six differently typed dark bars sent as a gift, and one author's Aldi price-and-quality favorites. No captured source supplies a blind matched taste result, exact delivered labels and lots, a verified current ten-to-twelve-dollar craft offer, or a universal price at which quality stops improving.",
